questions.question:
A `<div>` element has the following CSS rules applied:
```css
div {
  box-sizing: content-box;
  width: 250px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 5px solid black;
  margin: 15px;
}
```
What is the TOTAL rendered horizontal space (in pixels) that this element occupies on the web page (including outer margins)?

A developer attempts to position a 'Close' badge in the top-right corner of a card container:
```html
<div class="card">
  <button class="close-btn">X</button>
  <h3>Card Title</h3>
</div>
```
With CSS:
```css
.card {
  width: 300px;
  height: 200px;
}
.close-btn {
  position: absolute;
  top: 10px;
  right: 10px;
}
```
Instead of appearing in the top-right corner of the card, the button jumps to the very top-right corner of the ENTIRE browser window. What is the bug, and how is it fixed?

questions.scenario:
A student builds a 3-column course summary grid for the Nile University computer science department:
```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="card">COS 201</div>
  <div class="card">IFT 203</div>
  <div class="card">SEN 201</div>
</div>
```
With CSS:
```css
.container {
  width: 900px;
}
.card {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 300px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid #ccc;
}
```
The student expects all three cards to sit side-by-side in a single 900px row. Instead, each card drops onto a new line, stacking vertically.

On the Nile University Exam Portal, developers implement a sticky top navigation bar:
```css
.navbar {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 60px;
  background: #003366;
}
```
Two severe usability bugs emerge: (1) The top 60px of page content (the main heading `<h1>Student Exam Schedule</h1>`) is hidden underneath the fixed navbar when the page loads; (2) When a student opens a dropdown menu from the navbar, the dropdown disappears BEHIND an embedded course video player on the page.

