questions.answer:
1. Stacking Context & `z-index` Mechanics:
- `z-index` determines the 3-dimensional stacking order of elements perpendicular to the screen (the Z-axis). Elements with higher z-index values are rendered in front of elements with lower values.
- Critical Rule: `z-index` ONLY applies to positioned elements (elements where `position` is `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed`, or `sticky`). On default `position: static` elements, `z-index` is ignored by the browser.
- In Bug 2, the video player element created a separate hardware-accelerated stacking context, rendering in front of the un-elevated navbar dropdown.

2. The CSS Fix:
Assign a high z-index to the `.navbar` container (which already has `position: fixed;`):
```css
.navbar {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 60px;
  background: #003366;
  z-index: 1000; /* Guarantees navbar and its child dropdowns sit on top of all page elements */
}

.dropdown-menu {
  position: absolute;
  top: 60px;
  left: 0;
  z-index: 1001;
}
```

